doc.details:
This is the inbound side of URL handling; UrlOpener sends links out. The URL that launched the process is kept as the initial URL and is not repeated as an event, unless it arrived after GetInitialUrl() had already returned nullopt: the system can deliver it after a binding's startup code ran, and then it is emitted as well. Each URL delivered to the running process afterwards is emitted as a ProtocolUrlReceivedEvent. URLs that arrive while nothing listens are queued (at most 32, oldest dropped) and delivered to the first listener, so a listener added during startup does not miss a link. Events are emitted asynchronously on the UI thread.

Launch URLs arrive before most bindings load this library. Hosts have to capture them early: macOS and native C++ hosts call GetInstance() before the application finishes launching; Flutter apps depend on the protocol_handler plugin (leanflutter). The scheme itself is declared to the system by the application package (Info.plist CFBundleURLTypes, an Android intent filter, an MSIX uap:Protocol), or on unpackaged Windows apps written by Register().

```cpp
auto& handler = ProtocolHandler::GetInstance();
handler.Register("myapp");
if (auto url = handler.GetInitialUrl()) {
  // The app was launched by *url.
}
handler.AddListener<ProtocolUrlReceivedEvent>(
    [](const ProtocolUrlReceivedEvent& event) { auto url = event.GetUrl(); });
```

